of myself, but more

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.760
important the real customer
story of Ruben Kruit,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.190
CTO of Young Capital.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:22.010
Thank you very much for
joining us today, Ruben Kruit.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.940
So I am going to start
introducing to you

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.460
why the cloud has been
so important to Google

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.670
to achieving our goals,
and how we have applied it

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.040
in our day-to-day technology,
and how we collaborate

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.300
in order for other companies
to have advantages of that

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.210
as well.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.400
My name is Bas Rookhuijzen.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.010
I'm account executive
at Google Cloud.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.890
And I'm here today to help you
with any questions regarding

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.240
these topics.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.310
So what we want to achieve
with this cloud product

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.470
and what we're going
to elaborate on today

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.360
is we want to
bring teams closer,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.360
so that you can move faster and
go bigger than the competition.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.070
Actually what most
of the speakers

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.360
already confirmed at the
key note this morning,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.460
Bill Hippenmeyer
elaborated on that.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.470
But what's more
important than that alone

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.810
is why is that so
important right now.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.310
Why was the technology that
we used 5 or 10 years ago

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.410
sufficient enough to
stay ahead of the curve

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.380
and to communicate with each
other the way we were used to?

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.320
Why is that no longer
sufficient enough?

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.480
Why do we need to amend
faster than we should?

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.620
Because one reason, disruptive
technology-driven startups

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.100
are taking over the world.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.520
They're becoming more and
more relevant every day.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.690
And they become more
and more powerful.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.540
So we have to act like
disruptive technology-driven

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.680
startups to stay
ahead of the curve.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.850
We have been doing so
over the last 17 years.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.940
And we want to help
and inspire you,

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.010
hopefully that you can
cherry pick from our ideas

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.180
and move at the same pace.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.470
Culture is more important
even then technology alone.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.590
What we have learned
from startups

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.560
and from our own organization,
is that three things

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.610
are very important to remember.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.060
First of all, we need a very
strong mission statement,

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.009
how to organize the
world's information

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.676
and how to make it universally
accessible and useful.

00:02:12.676 --> 00:02:14.300
That's the mission
statement of Google,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.000
regardless where you work at
the self-driving car department,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.130
if you work at YouTube
or at Google Cloud.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.430
Everybody wants to organize
the world's information.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.140
That's our motivation.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.230
And we're looking for
people, partners, employees,

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.190
colleagues that want to
contribute to the same goal

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:30.690
as well.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.450
So that's our primary statement.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.480
And every company has its own
very relevant mission statement

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.690
to focus on.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.730
Secondly, transparency
helps us to move quickly.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.310
Open information,
everything is open.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.370
Everybody can access
information very fast.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:51.530
It's pivotal for
innovation processes.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.510
In order to innovate
with the same speed

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.060
as disruptive
technology-driven startups,

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.030
you need to have a very open and
transparent culture and a very

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.310
open and transparent
technology platform.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.540
That's why transparency
is so important.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.480
And thirdly, voice
is very important.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.710
So everybody in the
organization has the same power

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.200
to create an initiative,
to create products,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.390
to create a new project.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:19.490
And an example that I can give
you today is based on this URL,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:23.490
I would ask you to use if you
have questions for me today.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.740
This is a very
simple tool to use.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.380
You simply type in the URL.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.420
You'll access a site.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:30.500
You can ask a question.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.600
You'll see each
other's questions.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.140
And Ruben and myself will
answer the top three questions.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.570
Because you can like
each other's questions.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.420
And the question that
has most likes we

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.330
are committed to answer at
the end of this presentation.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.960
So that's something
that we use as well.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.620
Google has 60,000-70,000
employees in the meantime.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.760
We have a TGIF every week.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:56.990
You know, TGIF, right?

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.740
It's thank Google it's Friday.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:03.140
And on this Friday, we have a
presentation of Diane Greene,

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:07.460
our CEO; or Sundar,
or the group's CEO

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.550
presenting to us our new
strategy or new products,

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.090
financial numbers, and
thousands of people

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.960
are watching that live stream.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.510
We were used to sending
questions to those speakers

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.860
afterwards, or sending
it to the secretary.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.480
And well, the representation
of the best question

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.310
being answered on stage
wasn't always quite fair.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:32.090
So the question answered
was for the best interest

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:33.350
of the speaker in most times.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.380
So we designed this specific
tool for ourselves as well.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.000
We created the website.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.510
And colleagues can
like those questions.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.290
We think it's a
very democratic way

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:45.470
to get the best questions
rising to the top.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.650
Because the best idea
can come from anywhere.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.120
And that's the key
message that we

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.340
want to send in this
organization is the best

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.140
idea can come from
anywhere, can come

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.060
from the vice president
in your organization,

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:57.160
executive vice president.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.200
It can come from some high
potential during a workshop.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.070
Or most likely it can
come from somebody

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.950
who's in direct contact
with your customer,

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.500
feels the friction, and can
solve something right away.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:11.810
You want to give somebody that
power and the speed of movement

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.610
there right away.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.060
Well, this is what
enables the cloud.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.190
We have data centers
all over the world.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:23.900
We invest about $10 billion
per year in those data centers.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.550
Realizing this network,
with our own glass fiber

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:29.880
network crossing the planet.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.780
So we're the only company in
the world that has its own glass

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.120
fiber network across
the planet that

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.440
will give the
customers and the users

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.470
optimal performance of the apps
that you deploy on our network,

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:45.470
and also the apps that
you use, regardless

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.009
of whether you're in Tokyo,
or in Iowa, or in London,

00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:49.550
or wherever in the
world you're going

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.430
to have a very short latency
towards the information.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:55.489
Because we ship the information
with you wherever you go.

00:05:55.489 --> 00:05:57.530
And that helps you to get
a top-notch performance

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:58.488
with cloud performance.

00:05:58.488 --> 00:06:00.000
That's very important.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.080
So what is going wrong at
traditional companies today?

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.040
Why are companies like
Kodak, Blockbuster,

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.170
and numerous other
companies going down

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:12.840
based on competition from
disruptive technology-driven

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:13.340
startups?

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.500
It's all about culture.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.460
Firstly, they see information
as being top secret.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.550
It's very difficult to access
certain points of information.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.000
You need to ask for permission.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.450
You need a certain
clearance level.

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:29.810
You need a lot of
time and effort

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:32.490
spent to get to the
right information.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.310
And that's a disaster for your
innovation pace, of course.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.490
So your people in
your organization

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.590
who are in direct contact
with your customers,

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:42.119
if they take a lot
of time to figure out

00:06:42.119 --> 00:06:43.910
what the right and
relevant information is,

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.900
then you're already too
late in this world today.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:48.260
So you're too slow.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.740
Secondly what we see
is that making mistakes

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.470
is being perceived at
traditional companies

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:56.640
as being very, very expensive.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.710
So complete careers are
built upon, in fact,

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:01.710
making as few
mistakes as possible.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:05.570
So we think it's very
important that there

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:08.570
is a culture of
psychological safety.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:10.610
That people are not
afraid of making mistakes,

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:12.860
because in this digital
day and age making

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:15.650
mistakes should
not be expensive.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.620
And finally it's what
we call the fat cat.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:21.380
And the fat cat represents
the decision making power

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:22.940
lies in the hands of the few.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.400
In traditional companies
there are only a few people

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.630
in the organization that
have the power to say yes

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.330
to a certain initiative.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.420
And that's bad for two reasons.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.960
Number one, it takes a lot
of time to get on the agenda.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:38.360
Every Tuesday morning there's
a qualification analysis,

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.630
or a spend board,
or a review board.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.850
And secondly, it's a
disaster because people

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:46.700
blow their project
out of proportion.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.490
Perhaps they only need $5,000.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:52.690
But they suddenly project their
project to be worth tenfold

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:56.660
or a hundredfold in order to get
on the agenda of this fat cat,

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:57.530
as we call it.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.720
And that's also a disaster
for your innovation process.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:01.777
So what does work?

00:08:01.777 --> 00:08:03.110
We would like to share with you.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:06.170
First of all, those disruptive
technology-driven organizations

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.300
focus on all the data is online.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:09.890
Everything is in the cloud.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.460
You can access it everywhere.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.360
That's the nature of disruptive
technology-driven startups.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.540
They do not invest in
on-premise hardware,

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:21.560
do not invest in highly
specialized technology people

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:22.730
for the infrastructure part.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:26.550
They rely on the information
that's available in the cloud.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:29.060
Secondly, they build everything
primarily for mobile.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.190
Because mobile is
the primary user

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:34.460
interface of all your
users, all your consumers.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:37.789
Therefore, also
more relevant if you

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:39.890
build any app on
that platform, it's

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:42.620
going to need to
be mobile first,

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.650
especially because the
mobile phone doesn't

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:47.870
rely on the CPU alone
of the mobile phone.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:51.860
It primarily relies on the big
data centers, the Google Cloud

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.360
Platform, for instance.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.430
Who of you has ever used
an Uber app, for instance?

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:57.840
Most of you, right?

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.350
The Uber app doesn't rely on
the CPU power of your phone.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:03.230
It relies on the CPU power
of the cloud calculating

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.310
where the nearest cab
driver is, of course.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.020
And the possibilities
are limitless because

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:10.710
of this vast technology.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.920
So there are limitations
is in your own creativity.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.960
And the moment that you
block that creativity,

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.760
then you'll be slower
than the competition.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:19.310
And that's a disaster.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:20.300
We've seen it at Uber.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.060
We've seen it at Kodak.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:26.660
We've seen it a Blockbuster,
a mix of companies

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.710
that have affected the world.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:31.180
And it's going
faster and faster.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.870
And I like this slide a lot.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:36.080
It's the CB Insights slides.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.492
It's the Unicorn Club.

00:09:37.492 --> 00:09:39.200
You're part of the
Unicorn Club if you're

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.360
worth over a billion dollars.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.220
And what CB Insights
did, they said

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:46.040
that they projected this
on a time scale going back,

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:47.630
dating back way
further than this.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.600
But I focused on this part.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.160
Especially because
what you can see

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.860
is that the founding date is
projected on this timeline

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:56.360
here.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:01.850
And it's getting
increasingly crowded today,

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.940
mainly because of the fact
that it takes less time

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:06.930
to found a company that's
worth a billion dollars.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.730
And that supports the
statement of that the fact that

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.439
disruptive technology-driven
startups are very relevant.

00:10:12.439 --> 00:10:14.230
And that's our next
competition, regardless

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.890
of what segments you are.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:19.290
It's going faster and faster
because of Moore's law.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:21.370
And Moore's law, I don't
need to explain to you.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.540
But it's every 18 months
the CPU quality doubles,

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:27.720
and the price halves, right?

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:29.185
So it's 50% every 18 months.

00:10:29.185 --> 00:10:31.060
And it has been like
that since the invention

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:33.094
of the personal computer by IBM.

00:10:33.094 --> 00:10:35.510
And every year people say, OK,
it's not going to continue.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:36.176
It's impossible.

00:10:36.176 --> 00:10:37.180
It still happens.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:38.770
So that's why they keep--

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:42.290
because technology expressed
is so, so incredibly strong.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:46.580
So what we did at Google is
we translated our learning

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:50.980
over the last 17 years into
a couple of investment themes

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.230
that we have to be
aware of at the moment

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.270
that we are going to
be more productive.

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:57.940
First of all, we need
to focus on the teams.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:00.220
That's the most important
part, the culture part.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.320
We want to make a
tool set that allows

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:06.340
them to work real time to give
them the feeling that they're

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:09.662
working in small teams
together to foster

00:11:09.662 --> 00:11:11.620
these serendipitous
connections, as we call it.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.360
That's the source of innovation.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.550
Secondly, it needs
to be super secure.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.410
People need to get the
feeling that wherever

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.380
they work, on what device.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.820
There is no risk of
information getting leaked.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:26.020
Because that's a hygiene
factor number one.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:27.880
Thirdly, it's
beyond productivity.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.380
That means that
we're going to help

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.710
you do more with less time.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.430
And of course because we really
believe in the best idea can

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:38.620
come from anywhere, we
want to create a platform

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:42.180
where customers and
partners can build upon.

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:45.160
So that you can add
quality to the database.

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.660
We see that more apps
are built on our network

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:49.324
than we build ourselves.

00:11:49.324 --> 00:11:50.740
So the platform
is very important,

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:53.520
and usability is pivotal.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.990
And that's something that
Ruben will elaborate on

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:59.200
as well that if you want
to be able to apply it,

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.760
the adoption rate
needs to be high

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.410
and the usability
needs to be easy.

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:04.580
We call it a toothbrush rule.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.120
We optimize for growth,
not for revenue.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:08.560
And a toothbrush
rule means if you

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.720
develop something
that doesn't require

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.290
a manual for a billion people to
use they don't need to download

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:17.200
the manual first or
look at a YouTube movie

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:22.012
or enroll for training, then
the product is a success.

00:12:22.012 --> 00:12:23.470
That's for us, the
toothbrush rule.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:26.780
And that's why usability
is so important.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:29.290
These are the products
today that we have developed

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:31.150
based on this principle.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:32.562
It's not written in a vacuum.

00:12:32.562 --> 00:12:34.270
But these are the
products today that you

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:39.010
can use that we embedded this
knowledge of our culture in.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:40.670
This is what we
specifically needed

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:42.460
when we started to
build Google.com, when

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:46.420
we saw that the tools
that we had to communicate

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.140
were not sufficient enough,
were enough fast enough.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:54.820
They were too expensive and
required too much backbone

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:56.830
technologists to run on.

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:59.590
And that's why we
developed our own G Suite.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:02.200
And that's what we have brought
to the enterprise market

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:03.700
as well.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.650
Teams is for us the
most important part.

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.483
That's why I mentioned
it a couple of times.

00:13:07.483 --> 00:13:10.300
We want to work
teams in small teams.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.720
We have calculated, we have
estimated at the moment

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:17.830
that you work 3 meters apart
is going to be exponential

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:21.100
higher productivity than when
you work 18 meters apart,

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.020
let alone when you
work from home.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.660
So that's why we want
everybody, by default,

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.090
to work in the office together.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.430
That's not always
possible, because people

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:31.960
work across the globe.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.530
That's why we created products
with high-resolution images

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:39.640
like Hangout, to ensure people
to be able to work together,

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:42.350
as if they were working
next to each other.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.385
Hangout chat is a
product of that,

00:13:44.385 --> 00:13:47.470
that you have seen today as
well, where people can very

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.710
easily exchange information
and Hangout Meet

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:53.110
is a video conference service
embedded in your agenda,

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:55.420
embedded in the meeting
rooms, where you immediately

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:57.820
can include meeting
rooms, book meeting rooms,

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.840
and have the feeling that you're
together, even though there

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.350
is an ocean in between you.

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:08.845
Team Drives is something that
we have introduced recently

00:14:08.845 --> 00:14:11.350
that I encourage
you to check what's

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:12.940
possible with Team Drives.

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:15.280
Team Drives a folder
environment where

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:18.990
you're able to share
information with each other,

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:20.990
beyond your own ownership.

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.382
So in the past we
had a dry folder.

00:14:23.382 --> 00:14:24.340
And you were the owner.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.290
And the moment that
you left the company,

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:28.899
the documents were only to be
accessed by the administrator,

00:14:28.899 --> 00:14:29.440
for instance.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:32.110
Now we have Team
Drives that allows

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:35.469
you to use Drive as you
would have used fileshare,

00:14:35.469 --> 00:14:36.010
for instance.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:38.680
There is one folder
at the f drive,

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.770
and everybody can find
it, and it's easy.

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:46.600
Live updates on what
we do on Google sites,

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.790
so it's very easy to
create internet sites,

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:51.340
and update it all the time.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.030
And it's obviously very secure,
built on our own security

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.097
levels, like you have heard
this morning of Julien.

00:14:58.097 --> 00:14:59.930
So we have seven-- and
I heard this morning,

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:02.675
eight even products with
1 billion users each.

00:15:02.675 --> 00:15:04.420
So we have to be very secure.

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:05.832
so that's a hygiene
factor of us.

00:15:05.832 --> 00:15:07.540
We have many, many
questions that we get.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:08.410
Are you secure enough?

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:09.700
Because it's in
the cloud and it's

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.200
spread out all
over the world, how

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.300
do I know all my data is safe?

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.700
We're more than happy to go
in a conversation with you

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:16.270
in detail.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:17.680
But it's built up
because we have

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.200
our own proprietary hardware
we build around data centers.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.240
We have our own
glass fiber cables.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:24.730
We have control of
any aspect in the IT.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.390
And that why it's so super safe.

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:30.100
What we specifically
to want to focus on

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.710
is phishing, for instance.

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:36.100
That's what CIOs worry
about, mobile management data

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:37.480
breaches, or personal part.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:40.570
So CIOs usually believe that
we have the back end covered.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:42.400
But the front end is
more of a problem.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.240
How do you get your users safe?

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.050
And that's why we
have this program

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:50.640
to help users to be more
secure, automatically,

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:52.430
but for instance a security key.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:54.250
So you don't need
an OTP code anymore.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:55.990
It's simply something
that you have,

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:58.660
something that you know
embedded in one device.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.570
And then you're free to go.

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:03.560
Mobile device management
is very important.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:06.040
We've deployed that recently
with the entire Dutch National

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:06.890
Police Force.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:09.160
We are doing the mobile
device management for them.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:11.320
They haven't had a
security breach since then.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:14.230
So we're very happy to
have them as a partner,

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:18.900
as a reference for the future.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:23.740
Where we can help them
audit and edit the data

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.390
based on a configuration basis.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:28.210
And they no longer need
to technically update it

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:30.160
all the time.

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:32.140
First-party apps
are part of that,

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:35.290
and there is a platform
where everybody can built on.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:38.320
As a third party, you can
easily with API integration,

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:40.330
build your platforms
on top of that.

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:41.869
With the Gmail
add-ons for instance,

00:16:41.869 --> 00:16:43.410
you can integrate
it with other apps.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:48.080
And that might be very
useful for your environment.

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:52.960
Productivity is pivotal
for getting the most out

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:55.340
of your employees.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.870
We apply machine
learning to that

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:01.120
to increase the productivity.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.760
We help you with smart replies.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.715
We help you with setting a
reply already ready there

00:17:06.715 --> 00:17:09.190
at the moment that you
receive an email that you only

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:11.859
need to press OK and
the mail is gone.

00:17:11.859 --> 00:17:13.750
And it saves you a lot of time.

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:16.259
Find the time and calendar
is also an example that we

00:17:16.259 --> 00:17:18.310
are going to improve over time.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:23.749
It's already there, and there is
an interoperability possibility

00:17:23.749 --> 00:17:25.454
with the Microsoft
environment as well.

00:17:25.454 --> 00:17:27.120
It's going to be very
short presentation

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:28.270
